From 9bb9dffdaeb71df61000c7487a8b6f583ea529c2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: suiyan <suiyan@sunmmio.com>
Date: Wed, 22 Jul 2026 15:40:51 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] DADAO: register f32/f64 soft-float RTLIB libcalls (ML-020a)

DADAOISelLowering.cpp never registered any f32/f64 register class or
operation actions, so DADAO had no floating-point support at all. The
first time musl code performed a `double` comparison (vfprintf.c's
printf_core), LLVM's softenSetCCOperands legalization path tried to
emit a standard GNU soft-float libcall (e.g. __eqdf2/__gedf2) and hit
`report_fatal_error: unsupported library call operation`, because no
target had ever told the new RTLIB::LibcallImpl infrastructure
(llvm/include/llvm/IR/RuntimeLibcalls.td) that these libcalls exist
for the dadao triple.

Add a DADAOSystemLibrary entry (matching the minimal pattern used by
other software-floating-point-only targets such as Lanai) that maps
DefaultRuntimeLibcallImpls -- the standard GNU-style names
(__adddf3/__eqdf2/__floatsidf/... ) -- to any triple with
Triple::getArch() == Triple::dadao. This requires no DADAOISelLowering.cpp
changes: DADAO registers no f32/f64 register class, so these types
remain "illegal" and the existing generic SelectionDAG type-legalizer
softening path picks them up automatically once a libcall name is
available -- exactly as it does for Lanai/MSP430/plain RV32I.

Verified: `report_fatal_error: unsupported library call operation` is
completely eliminated. `vfscanf.o` (previously failing on this error)
now compiles clean. `vfprintf.o` and roughly 100 math/complex .o files
that previously failed on the same error now progress further into
instruction selection/scheduling and hit a separate, pre-existing
SelectionDAG glue-chain defect already tracked in docs/issues.yaml
(musl-backend-assert-illegal-result-number /
musl-backend-assert-node-already-inserted, first seen 2026-07-17/
ML-010a, related to the deferred DL-063c indirect-call scheduler
investigation) -- not introduced by this change, just reached far
more often now that floating-point code type-legalizes successfully.

No DADAOISelLowering.cpp/DADAOSubtarget.cpp/DADAOTargetMachine.cpp
changes were needed; the fix is entirely in the shared, cross-target
llvm/include/llvm/IR/RuntimeLibcalls.td (same file every other target's
SystemRuntimeLibrary entry lives in).
---
 llvm/include/llvm/IR/RuntimeLibcalls.td | 16 ++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+)

diff --git a/llvm/include/llvm/IR/RuntimeLibcalls.td b/llvm/include/llvm/IR/RuntimeLibcalls.td
index e7d636841c4b..2cf373126413 100644
--- a/llvm/include/llvm/IR/RuntimeLibcalls.td
+++ b/llvm/include/llvm/IR/RuntimeLibcalls.td
@@ -2699,6 +2699,22 @@ def AVRSystemLibrary
               // Trigonometric rtlib functions
               avr_sin, avr_cos)>;
 
+//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
+// DADAO Runtime Libcalls
+//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
+
+// DADAO has no floating-point registers/instructions (contracts/isa/spec.md):
+// all f32/f64 arithmetic, comparison, and int<->float conversion is lowered
+// to the standard GNU-style soft-float libcalls (__adddf3, __eqdf2, etc, all
+// members of DefaultRuntimeLibcallImpls). This mirrors Lanai's minimal
+// soft-float-only SystemRuntimeLibrary entry below; DADAO does not implement
+// a stack-protector feature so __stack_chk_fail/__stack_chk_guard are
+// intentionally omitted (unlike Lanai).
+def isDADAO : RuntimeLibcallPredicate<"TT.getArch() == Triple::dadao">;
+
+def DADAOSystemLibrary
+    : SystemRuntimeLibrary<isDADAO, (add DefaultRuntimeLibcallImpls)>;
+
 //===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
 // DXIL Runtime Libcalls
 //===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
-- 
